WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.100
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.970
With the nimble progress we are seeing in
computer graphics research, it is now not

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:16.110
only possible to perform beautiful fluid simulations,
but we can also simulate more advanced effects,

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:22.910
such as honey coiling, ferrofluids climbing
up on other objects, and a variety of similar

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.120
advanced effects.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:30.470
However, due to the complexity of these techniques,
we often have to wait for several seconds,

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:35.660
or even minutes for every single one of these
images, which often means that we have to

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.320
leave our computer crunching these scenes
overnight.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.210
Or even wait several days for the results
to appear.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.680
But what about real-time applications?

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.870
Can we perform these fluid simulations in
a more reasonable timeframe?

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:56.649
Well, this technique offers detailed fluid
simulations, like the one here, and is blazing

00:00:56.649 --> 00:00:57.950
fast.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:04.389
The reason for this is that one, it uses a
sparse volume representation and two, it supports

00:01:04.389 --> 00:01:08.200
parallel computation and runs on your graphics
card.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.759
So what do these terms really mean?

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:13.079
Let’s start with the sparse part.

00:01:13.079 --> 00:01:17.439
With classical fluid simulation techniques,
the simulation domain has to be declared in

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:21.299
advance and is typically confined to a cube.

00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:23.340
This comes with several disadvantages.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:29.351
For instance, if we wish to have a piece of
fluid or smoke coming out of this cube, we

00:01:29.351 --> 00:01:30.719
are out of luck.

00:01:30.719 --> 00:01:36.029
The simulation domain stays, so we would have
to know in advance how the simulation pans

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:38.219
out, which we don’t.

00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:43.739
Now, the first thing you are probably thinking
about, well, of course, make the simulation

00:01:43.739 --> 00:01:45.090
domain bigger!

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:51.149
Yes, but unless special measures are taken,
the bigger the domain, the more we have to

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:52.299
compute.

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:55.100
Even the empty parts take some computation!

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.259
Ouch.

00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:01.109
This means that we have to confine the simulation
to as small a domain as we can.

00:02:01.109 --> 00:02:06.439
So, this is where this technique comes into
play - the sparse representation that it uses

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:12.620
means that the simulation domain can take
any form, as you see here, it just starts

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.310
altering the shape of the simulation domain
as the fluid splashes out of it.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:22.659
Furthermore, we are not only not doing work
in the empty parts of the domain, which is

00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:27.060
a huge efficiency increase, but we don’t
need to allocate too much additional memory

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:31.980
for these regions, which, you will see in
a minute, will be a key part of the value

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.370
proposition of this technique.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:40.010
We noted that it supports parallel computation
and runs on your graphics card.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:45.400
The graphics card part is key, because otherwise,
it would run on your processor, like most

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.610
of the techniques that require “minutes
per frame”.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.989
The more complex the technique is, typically,
the more likely that it runs on your processor,

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:58.239
which has a few cores to a few tens of cores.

00:02:58.239 --> 00:03:04.269
However, your graphics card, comparably, is
almost a supercomputer as it has up to a few

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:08.700
hundred, or, even a few thousand cores to
compute on.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.620
So why not use that?

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.329
Well, it’s not that simple, and here is
where the word “parallel” is key.

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:21.930
If the problem can be decomposed into smaller,
independent problems, they can be allocated

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:26.930
to many-many cores that can work independently,
and much more efficiently.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:31.389
This, is exactly what this paper does with
the fluid simulation.

00:03:31.389 --> 00:03:36.959
It runs it on your graphics card, and hence,
it is typically 10 to 20 times faster than

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:40.040
the equivalent techniques running on your
processor.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.700
Let me try to demonstrate this with an example.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.580
Let’s talk about coffee.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.219
Making coffee is not a very parallel task.

00:03:48.219 --> 00:03:53.040
If you ask a person to make coffee, it can
typically be done in a few seconds.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.420
However, if you suddenly put 30 people in
the kitchen and ask them to make coffee, it

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:04.170
not only will not be a faster process, but
may even be slower than one person because

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:10.779
of two reasons: one, it is hard to coordinate
30 people and there will be miscommunication,

00:04:10.779 --> 00:04:15.849
and two, there are very few tools, and lots
of people, so they won’t be able to help

00:04:15.849 --> 00:04:19.780
each other, or much worse, will just hold
each other up.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:25.241
If we could formulate the coffee making problem
such that we need 30 units of coffee and we

00:04:25.241 --> 00:04:30.740
have 30 kitchens, we could just place one
person into each kitchen and then, they could

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:33.430
work efficiently and independently.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:39.200
At the risk of oversimplifying the situation,
this is an intuition of what this technique

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.000
does, and hence, it runs on your graphics
card, and is incredibly fast.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.870
Also, note that your graphics card typically
has a limited amount of memory, and, remember,

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:56.440
we noted that the sparse representation makes
it very gentle on memory usage, making this

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:02.290
the perfect algorithm for creating detailed,
large-scale fluid simulations quickly.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:03.310
Excellent design.

